Points to remember

1. Dot notation (table.column) exists because two tables can share column names. Once you JOIN, SQL needs to know exactly which table a column is coming from — especially for something like customer_id, which exists in both customers and orders.

2. Aliases are 100% my own choice. FROM customers c just means "call this table c for the rest of the query." Could be any word, not tied to the table's first letter. AS is optional — customers c and customers AS c work the same.

3. SELECT controls which columns show. JOIN controls which rows show. These are two separate decisions. Writing SELECT * doesn't change which rows survive a JOIN — that's purely down to the JOIN type and the ON condition.

4. INNER JOIN → only rows that match in both tables. Unmatched rows (like the Tablet order, customer_id 999) are dropped entirely.

5. LEFT vs RIGHT — decided by writing order, not the tables themselves. Whatever table comes right after FROM = LEFT. Whatever comes after JOIN = RIGHT. Swapping the order flips which side is "guaranteed."

6. LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table, matched or not. Unmatched columns from the right table come back as NULL. Unmatched right-side rows (like Tablet) get dropped, same as INNER JOIN.

7. RIGHT JOIN is the mirror — keeps every row from the right table. This is how the Tablet order (no matching customer) finally shows up — with NULL in the customer's name.

8. NULL is not the same as an empty string. Can never check = "" or = NULL to find a missing match. Must use IS NULL.

9. Self-JOIN = joining a table to itself, using two aliases. Used to compare rows within one table (e.g. customers who share the same city or age).

10. Without excluding self-matches, everyone matches themselves. a.customer_id <> b.customer_id stops someone from pairing with themselves — but still lets reversed duplicate pairs through (John–Peter AND Peter–John).

11. Using > (or <) instead of <> solves both problems at once. Blocks self-matches AND removes reversed duplicate pairs, since only one direction of the comparison can be true.

Where I'm at

JOIN logic (which rows survive, why) is solid. Remaining slip-ups were syntax-level — mixing up JOIN types per question, IS NULL vs = "" — not the core concepts.

